{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794743977,"nonce":"z/nFbWiRSFQjGHoEnGNcUlATEQ4=","message":"Type table from the TAST, checked against genhl (70fd003e, 2b374332, 5f62095f).\n\nPurpose, as the maintainer put it: use the TAST to build a faster runtime on the architecture already reached with HashLink as the input. The runtime, tiers and AIR stay; nAIR feeds them what the bytecode never carried. The type table is plumbing for that. It has to equal what the runtime reads, so it is checked against the same program compiled by genhl.\n\nWhat exists: crate `ash_nair` computes the layout of every class, enum and interface from a TAST file (field order, method slots, interface fields, anonymous structures, optional arguments, abstract unwrapping, class-level @:hlNative). `layout_check program.hl program.tast` compares it with the table a .hl carries; `tests/layout_vs_genhl.rs` does it for two fixtures.\n\nResult on the Haxe unit suite (compile-hl.hxml): 4259 of 4260 class and enum layouts equal genhl's, field for field and slot for slot. 56 more types are closure environments genhl creates only while compiling function bodies; those wait for body lowering.\n\nThe one difference is deliberate. `abstract Rec(Array\u003cRec\u003e)` as a field type: genhl makes `Array\u003cRec\u003e` an ArrayObj through an accident of OCaml physical equality (the abstract's inner reference is a different value from the outer one, so it unrolls one extra level). We give ArrayDyn, which is the element type the recursion actually implies.\n\nThree exporter bugs found by the comparison and fixed:\n- the OVERRIDE field flag was defined but never written, so no method had a virtual slot;\n- two generic methods that each declare a `T` shared one class record, so the second got the first's constraints (Array\u003cT:String\u003e and Array\u003cT:Float\u003e came out the same);\n- a typedef whose target was still a lazy type fell through to the branch that registers it after recursing, so `typedef Rec = {field:Rec}` reached through a generic function was cut at the depth guard. All 13 \"infinitely expanding typedef\" cases in the unit suite were this.\n\nAlso added to the format earlier in this series: abstract underlying types, statics objects, constant type arguments (format 3), and method flags on structure fields.\n\nNext, in order of what the TAST buys: lower function bodies for a subset (ints, floats, strings, arrays, classes, statics), run it on the existing tiers, and measure against the .hl path on the rows we trail (spectralnorm, fasta, knucleotide, binarytrees). The first passes that need TAST facts the bytecode lacks: closed-world devirtualization (final and non-overridden receivers), constructor scalar replacement without inlining, Null\u003cT\u003e and null-check elimination, and array bounds facts.","files":null}]}